WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Secure ASP.NET Core Web API engineering is a repeatable workflow.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:12.200
It starts with clear endpoint purpose, expected callers, trust boundaries, and exposure decisions.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:24.780
It continues through routing, middleware order, environment configuration, validation, identity, authorization, data access, resource protection, and operational monitoring.

4
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.740
The parts are connected.

5
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.880
DTO shape affects over-posting risk.

6
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.860
Model binding affects what the server accepts.

7
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.440
Validation affects data access.

8
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:42.260
Authentication establishes identity context, but authorization decides what that identity may do.

9
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.000
CORS influences browser exposure, but it does not replace access control.

10
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.220
Rate limits and resource bounds keep services reliable under real caller behavior.

11
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.660
Security continues after release.

12
00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:08.520
Safe errors, structured logs, correlation identifiers, controlled OpenAPI exposure, dependency review, CI/CD protection, and runtime maintenance determine whether the team can operate the API responsibly.

13
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:15.740
Production feedback should inform code review and future design, not sit disconnected in an operations tool.

14
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.420
The practical path is to make good API habits ordinary.

15
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:34.680
Keep endpoints narrow, shape DTOs intentionally, validate on the server, authorize at the resource level, limit expensive work, protect tokens and secrets, sanitize logs, and review deployment configuration.

16
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.260
Small decisions repeated across endpoints are what reduce recurring production risk.

17
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.500
A secure API is one a team can explain.

18
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:57.480
Who can call it, what it accepts, what it returns, how it fails, what it logs, how it is documented, and how it is maintained should be visible enough for developers, reviewers, and operators to reason about together.
